/*
 * The roster of MIR nodes - one row per `mir::Node::Kind`, and the only place a kind is written
 * down. The enum, the concrete-type cast in `visitNode`, the mnemonic and the traits are all
 * generated from it, so a kind cannot be added without deciding every column for it.
 *
 * The difference from the current resolver's inst.def is what a missing answer does.
 * `Analysis-IR.md` §1.3 is about ownership effects defaulting to *absence*, which is the unsafe
 * direction: an instruction that forgot to declare a transfer looked exactly like an ordinary read.
 * Here there is no ownership column at all, because ownership is not a property of a kind - it is a
 * `UseMode` on each operand, supplied when the node is built and refused by the builder if it is
 * missing. What is left in this table is structure, and `verifyBody` checks that a node's declared
 * structure and its stored fields agree.
 *
 * ## The columns
 *
 *  - `kind` is the `mir::Node::Kind` enumerator, and the order of these rows is the order of the enum.
 *  - `Struct` is the concrete type a `Node` of that kind may be cast to; `visitNode` is generated
 *    from this column, so several kinds may share one struct where they are one node with two
 *    meanings.
 *  - `mnemonic` is what a dump prints for the kind. A node that refines it from its own fields does
 *    so in print.cpp; what is here is the base name.
 *  - `flags` is what a pass may ask about a *kind* rather than about a node:
 *      - `kMirResult` - defines a value another node may name by ID, and is what licenses the cast
 *        to `NodeResult` that reaches its occurrence. Its absence is checkable and structural: a
 *        terminator or a store is not an operand source, does not derive from `NodeResult`, and
 *        therefore does not carry twelve bytes of result type it has no result for.
 *      - `kMirTerminator` - ends its block, and is the only kind that may name successors.
 *
 * There were three columns and there are two. The one that went was `kMirSuspends`, and it is worth
 * recording why rather than quietly dropping it: whether a call may suspend the enclosing fiber is a
 * property of the *callee's published summary* (Analysis-Sharing.md §3.3), not of the fact that the
 * node is a call. A kind-level bit can answer only "every call suspends" or "no call does", both of
 * which are wrong, and the across-suspend rule needs the per-call answer to have anything to check
 * live loans against. It is `Summary::suspension` on the selected callee, read through the call node.
 *
 * ## `GenValue` is not a constant
 *
 * `fn (n: I32) width() -> I32 = n` reads a number the body will not know until it is instantiated,
 * so the node names the **parameter** and never a value. Under a specialization the conversion
 * substitutes it and finds a `ConstType`, which is where it becomes a literal; in the erased form
 * it is a load from a const slot. Folding it into `Const` would have meant a constant that is not
 * one, which is the state every reader of `Body::constants` would then have to test for.
 *
 * ## `Argument` is a node
 *
 * A block's parameters are `Argument` nodes held in `Block::arguments` rather than a second list of
 * bare occurrences, so an operand naming one names it the way it names any other value - by node ID,
 * through `SourceKind::Result`. The alternative was a fourth source kind meaning "argument N of the
 * block this operand is written in", and it is worse in exactly the way this representation is
 * meant to avoid: an operand whose meaning depends on where it is standing. They are the one node
 * kind that is not in a block's `nodes` list, because they have no position among the instructions -
 * they hold on entry.
 *
 * ## The mnemonic is a base name
 *
 * `binary`, `unary` and `write` each print something narrower than their row says - `add`, `neg`,
 * `write.init` - and print.cpp is where that happens. What is in this column is what the kind is
 * called when nothing narrower is known, which is what a verifier's diagnostic wants.
 */

//            kind          struct            mnemonic          flags
YANA_MIR_INST( Argument,     NodeArgument,     "argument"_v,     kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Const,        NodeConst,        "const"_v,        kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( GenValue,     NodeGenValue,     "gen.value"_v,    kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Read,         NodeRead,         "read"_v,         kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Binary,       NodeBinary,       "binary"_v,       kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Unary,        NodeUnary,        "unary"_v,        kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Aggregate,    NodeAggregate,    "aggregate"_v,    kMirResult )
/*
 * A **window** over a contiguous container - `[T]` in a parameter, and `xs[a..b]`.
 *
 * It reshapes a *borrow*, and that is the whole design: its operand is the value a `read` of the
 * container's place produced, so the loan is the read's and this node creates none. What comes out
 * is the same storage described differently - `Array(T)` with `AccessForm::SequenceWindow` - which
 * is Analysis-Borrows-Revised.md §3.1's adaptation: "such adaptation does not make the donor's
 * logical type equal to `Array(T)` and does not specialize the callee".
 *
 * A node and not a conversion between types, because the *logical type* genuinely changes: a
 * `[T *n]` donor states its length and a window does not, so what a caller hands over is two words
 * where the callee cannot know a number. What does **not** change is who owns the storage, which is
 * why the operand is `Inspect` and the result carries the borrow's own contract.
 *
 * `from` and `count` are always present. `total(xs)` is `xs[0 .. length]` written by the compiler
 * rather than by the author, and a node with optional operands would have been two shapes for every
 * consumer to learn - for a fixed array both fold to constants and cost nothing.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( Window,       NodeWindow,       "window"_v,       kMirResult )

/*
 * How many elements a window holds - `length(xs)`.
 *
 * A node rather than a field read, because a window is not a record: its two words are a
 * *manifestation* of `Array(T)` under `AccessForm::SequenceWindow`, and a projection naming field
 * one of it would be this stage knowing a layout it is not allowed to know.
 *
 * `Builder::addLength` answers with the number the window was **built** from where there is one,
 * so `length` on an adapted fixed array is a constant rather than a load - which is what
 * `doc/spec/collections.md` promises about `[U8 *64]` and is what makes every bounds test inside
 * such a callee fold.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( Length,       NodeLength,       "length"_v,       kMirResult )

/*
 * A function as a **value** - `let f = add`, a lambda, and Analysis-IR.md §11.4's G7.
 *
 * A node of its own rather than a constant, even though a named function's is one: a function value
 * is two words and address-only, so it is built into storage the way an aggregate is, and the
 * constant table in this stage holds scalars a register can carry. What it names is the declaration
 * and, for a lambda that captured anything, the environment its captures live in; what the lowering
 * makes of the pair is in `lower_fun.cpp`.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( FunValue,     NodeFunValue,     "fun.value"_v,    kMirResult )

/*
 * The raw address of the storage a loan names - `addressOf(x)`, and the only producer of a pointer
 * this stage has.
 *
 * It **reshapes a borrow and takes none of its own**, exactly as `Window` above does, and for the
 * same reason: the loan is the `read`'s, so the operand is `Inspect` and the storage stays live for
 * the loan's extent rather than for a lifetime this node would have to invent. That is what makes
 * `addressOf`'s declaration matter - `fn addressOf(it: source'a) -> %source'a` publishes the
 * relationship, so the pointer is an ordinary member of `source`'s origin set and every walk that
 * already computes an extent computes this one. A declaration written `it: a` would hand back an
 * address rooted in nothing, which is Analysis-Lens.md §12.3(c)'s use-after-free with nothing to
 * report it.
 *
 * **The pointer itself is an ordinary owned scalar.** Nothing about it is checked afterwards - a
 * raw pointer carries no validity, no initialization and no exclusivity, and `doc/spec/native.md`
 * is where that stops. What the origin preserves is *liveness* and nothing else.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( Address,      NodeAddress,      "address"_v,      kMirResult )

/*
 * One measurement of one type - the rows of metric.def, and the whole of `TypeTraits(a)`.
 *
 * A node rather than a constant, because at a *type parameter* it is not one: a body compiled once
 * reads its metrics out of the descriptor its caller supplied, and a number folded here would be
 * this stage answering a layout question it is not entitled to answer. That is the same division
 * `Place::Field` is under - the projection names an index and the manifestation says where it sits -
 * so this names a type and `resolve_ssa` turns it into either an immediate or a load.
 *
 * One node for five questions rather than five nodes, because the *shape* is what they share and it
 * is an unusual shape: no operands at all, `kMirResult`, and an answer that depends on a table the
 * caller supplied. What differs between them is one column, which is what a column is for.
 *
 * **The stride is not the size**, which is the one thing about walking storage a reader has to get
 * right: a record's trailing padding belongs to whoever contains it, so the size is what a
 * whole-value copy moves and the stride is what the *next* element starts at. `data P {a: I64, b:
 * U8}` is nine bytes and sixteen apart, and arithmetic in sizes overlaps its own elements.
 *
 * `of` is the type being measured and is never the result's: the result is a number or a `Bool`, and
 * which one is the position's business.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( Metric,       NodeMetric,       "metric"_v,       kMirResult )

/*
 * An operation the machine has and this IR does not - a system call, or a block of memory moved or
 * filled. The rows are native.def's.
 *
 * **A node and not a call**, because there is no function: what runs is an instruction the backend
 * emits, so there is no callee to name, no signature to check arguments against and no body for a
 * summary to walk. The declarations that reach it are `@builtin` in `Native`, and their signatures
 * are what checked the arguments before this node was built.
 *
 * `kMirResult` with a result that may be unit - `setMemory` answers `{}` and a system call answers
 * the kernel's number. Every operand is `Inspect`: these take addresses and counts, and an address
 * is a value the caller already had. Nothing here is checked, which is what `Native` is - the loans
 * that keep the storage alive are the ones the argument expressions took.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( Native,       NodeNative,       "native"_v,       kMirResult )

YANA_MIR_INST( Call,         NodeCall,         "call"_v,         kMirResult )
YANA_MIR_INST( Write,        NodeWrite,        "write"_v,        0 )
YANA_MIR_INST( Return,       NodeReturn,       "return"_v,       kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Jump,         NodeJump,         "jump"_v,         kMirTerminator )

/*
 * `break` out of the loop a continuation region *is* - the third of §10.1's four exits, and the one
 * that needs a kind of its own.
 *
 * A `NodeJump` in every respect, sharing its struct because it is one node with two meanings: it
 * leaves the region for the same block falling out leaves it for, which is where the caller carries
 * on either way. What the kind carries is the half the target cannot - falling out of an iterator's
 * body means "give me the next element" and `break` means "stop" - so the two are one edge in the
 * caller's control flow and two rows of the exit signal below it.
 *
 * `continue` has no row: ending an iteration *is* falling out, so it is an ordinary `Jump`.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( Break,        NodeJump,         "break"_v,        kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( LensCall,     NodeLensCall,     "lens.call"_v,    kMirTerminator )

/*
 * A call of a lens that declared `| E` - the same node with a second edge, and the second kind that
 * shares a struct for the reason `Break` does.
 *
 * A skipping callee may finish *without ever having handed over* - Analysis-Lens.md §7.1 - and there
 * is then no region for control to have gone through and come back from, so the caller needs a
 * second successor the way it needs a first. Which is all this adds: `to` is the region's entry as
 * before, and `otherwise` is where control resumes when the traversal failed.
 *
 * **What it failed with arrives as a block argument on that edge**, exactly as the hand-over arrives
 * on the region's. That is what keeps this a terminator with no result: a value defined by a
 * terminator would be defined on both edges and initialized on one, and a block parameter says what
 * is actually true - the reason exists on the path that has one.
 */
YANA_MIR_INST( LensTry,      NodeLensCall,     "lens.try"_v,     kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Branch,       NodeBranch,       "branch"_v,       kMirTerminator )
YANA_MIR_INST( Unreachable,  NodeUnreachable,  "unreachable"_v,  kMirTerminator )
